messages.body_plain:
If anyone is interested in auto-dif for literally
computing Hessians, the way to go is to write a templated
forward-mode auto dif.  (I still haven't gotten my head
around how a Hessian-vector product can be done more
easily.)

This is MUCH MUCH easier than reverse-mode auto-dif
in that it doesn't require any malloc.

Reverse-mode propagates the chain rule back from
the dependent variable to all of its subexpressions,
including the independent variables we care about.

Forward-mode propagates derivatives forward from a
single independent variable to all of the expressions
that depend on it.

The basic type is:

    struct fvar {
        fvar(double val) : _val(val), _adj(1.0) { }
        fvar(double val, double adj) : _val(val), _adj(adj) { }
        double _val;
        double _adj;
    }

And then operators just push the auto-dif through one
step of the chain rule w.r.t. the operation:

    // d/dx (x + b) = 1
    fvar operator+(fvar& a, double bv) {
        return fvar(a._val + bv,
                    a._adj * 1);
    }

    // d/dx (x * b) = b
    fvar operator*(fvar& a, double bv) {
        return fvar(a._val * bv,
                    a._adj * bv);
    }

    // d/dx sqrt(x) = 1/(2 * sqrt(x))
    fvar sqrt(fvar& a) {
        return fvar(sqrt(a._val),
                    a._adj * (1 / (2 * sqrt(a._val))));

and so on.

The next step is to template out the double, so you have:

    template <typename T>
    struct fvar {
        fvar(T val) : _val(val), _adj(1.0) { }
        fvar(T val, T adj) : _val(val), _adj(adj) { }
        T _val;
        T _adj;
    }

You might want a reference there for (const T& val).

The templated form of operators looks like:

  template <typename T>
  fvar operator*(fvar<T>& a, double bv) {
        return fvar<T>(a._val * bv,
                       a._adj * bv);
    }

A suitable T will define constructors and assignments
for doubles (making T(1) valid) and also for arithmetic.

For example, stan::agrad::var is such a T.

You then compute the Hessian of f:R^N -> R^N
a column at a time by moving through the arguments,
and for each one, using a forward-mode auto-dif
variable templated on stan::agrad::var.  Take
the result, grab its adj_ value, and then calculate
its gradient by a call to reverse mode auto dif.

The forward-mode part of this is cheap enough that
the cost shouldn't be noticeably more than running
K reverse-mode auto difs.

- Bob

On Tuesday, April 3, 2012 4:11:34 PM UTC-4, Marcus Brubaker wrote:
> I actually think a CovMatrix class is a good idea since they are such an integral part of almost any model.
>
> My main point was that if we want the model code to do the right thing we either a) need to figure out from the model specification which representation to use when parameterizing the model (I think this is going to be hard and error prone for everything but really simple cases) or have the user tell us, either by hinting when defining a cov_matrix parameter, or by requiring to the user to build the covariance matrix themselves in the model (what I had proposed).

I agree. The only difference is that I was thinking something along these lines

Case 1:

parameters {
  cov_matrix(N) Sigma;
}
model {
   Sigma ~ wishart();
  // something with Sigma
}

In this situation, we should use a plain Cholesky parameterization behind the scenes without the user knowing it or having to specify it explicitly. The C++ constructor for the CovMatrix would only initialize the L attribute.

In other words, the following more explicit version should be legal, but we shouldn't force the user to do

parameters {
  chol_factor(N) L;
}
transformed parameters {
  cov_matrix(N) Sigma;
  Sigma <- L * L'
}
model {
   Sigma ~ wishart();
  // something with Sigma
}

But if the user wants to do LKJ, then along the lines of what you said

Case 2:

parameters {
  vector CPCs(N * (N - 1));
  vector sds(N);
}
transformed parameters {
  cov_matrix(N) Sigma;
  Sigma <- LKJ_transformation(CPCs, sds);
}
model {
   double eta;
   eta <- 1.0;
   CPCs ~ LKJ_prior(eta);
   for(i in 1:N) {
     sds[i] ~ something();
   }
   // something with Sigma
}

Again, the Cholesky factorization is hidden from the user but integral to the C++.

Ben

Or Case 3 for an AR1 situation:

parameters {
  double rho(-1,1);
  double variance(0,);
}
transformed parameters {
  cov_matrix(N) Sigma;
  Sigma <- AR1(rho, variance);
}
model {
  rho ~ uniform(-1,1)
  variance ~ something();
   // something with Sigma
}

In the AR1 case, it is usually better *not* to calculate a Cholesky factor because the covariance matrix, its inverse (which is tridiagonal), and its determinant are known in closed form as a function of rho and variance. So, the CovMatrix constructor in C++ might initialize these three things, but not L or L_inverse. But whatever density function is using Sigma doesn't need to care much about about that. It just needs to check whether the desired attributed is initialized before it tries to use it in a calculation.

Ben

