The problem with the T,T template is that if
you give it (int,double), it won't match or if
you give it (var,double).

The problem with the arithmetic expression way
of doing things is that it does eager evaluation
and winds up putting too much on the auto-dif stack.

I went so far as to use if_else instead of the
ternary op to make the promote-to-var decision
lazy.

We really want to minimize the number of expressions
involving var (params, transformed params, local
vars in models).

So here's what I went with:

   inline double if_else(bool c, double y_true, double y_false) {

       return c ? y_true : y_false;
   }

   inline var if_else(bool c, const var& y_true, const var&y_false) {

      return c ? y_true : y_false;
   }

   inline var if_else(bool c, double y_true, const var& y_false) {
     if (c)
       return var(y_true);
     else
       return y_false;
   }

   inline var if_else(bool c, const var& y_true, const double y_false) {
     if (c)
       return y_true;
     else
       return var(y_false);
   }

I added unit tests for all the ways it's legal to
call from Stan.

- Bob

Jiqiang was right -- I was double counting
the constraint and shouldn't have included
the b ~ uniform(a,1) statement.

The right probability function for a uniform
distribution over a, b such that 0 < a < b < 1 is:

   p(a,b) = 2 * I(0 < a && a < b && b < 1)

I generalized Stan's transforms to allow
params (not pushed yet), so that the following
model implements the constant density with
constraint 0 < a < b < 1.

   parameters {
     real(0,1) a;
     real(a,1) b;
   }
   model {
   }

That's it.  Nothing in the model because the
density p(a,b) is constant.  The parameters
take care of the constraint (and Jacobian).

We can use this same idiom for constraint encoding
for the Jama model from BUGS Vol 3.

I also coded the JAGS-style model (at least I
think I recall seeing this in JAGS) with sorting
rather than circular DAGs:

   parameters {
     real(0,1) t1;
     real(0,1) t2;
   }
   transformed parameters {
     real(0,1) a;
     real(a,1) b;
     a <- fmin(t1,t2);
     b <- fmax(t1,t2);
   }
   model {
   }

